1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,752
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,772 --> 00:00:08,783
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,183 --> 00:00:13,883
This is Lecture 3, the needle-bar mechanism, a slider-crank.

4
00:00:15,403 --> 00:00:16,703
Here's the problem.

5
00:00:17,103 --> 00:00:23,986
A factory's lockstitch machines have a needle-bar crank radius of 15.5 millimetres,

6
00:00:24,006 --> 00:00:28,893
a 55-millimetre link and a reciprocating mass of 120 grams,

7
00:00:28,913 --> 00:00:30,723
an illustrative value.

8
00:00:31,123 --> 00:00:40,113
They run at 5000 r/min; production wants 6000.

9
00:00:40,513 --> 00:00:45,991
Maintenance has set a limit for the needle-bar bushings:

10
00:00:46,011 --> 00:00:52,275
no more than 800 newtons of inertia force at bottom dead centre,

11
00:00:52,295 --> 00:00:55,613
a figure assumed for this problem.

12
00:00:56,013 --> 00:00:59,473
What is the force at 6000?

13
00:00:59,873 --> 00:01:01,613
Is it over?

14
00:01:02,013 --> 00:01:04,473
And if so, what can we do?

15
00:01:05,993 --> 00:01:08,104
What it must do, and how it's built.

16
00:01:09,624 --> 00:01:12,725
The mechanism has to meet five requirements at once.

17
00:01:13,125 --> 00:01:17,240
Enough stroke: room for the cloth at the top,

18
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:21,099
the eye below the hook path at the bottom.

19
00:01:21,499 --> 00:01:27,159
Exact timing: bottom dead centre at 180 degrees.

20
00:01:27,559 --> 00:01:36,259
High speed: at 5000 r/min the bar reverses more than eighty times a second.

21
00:01:36,659 --> 00:01:37,519
Low vibration.

22
00:01:37,919 --> 00:01:43,645
And the bar must not turn, because the scarf has to face the hook,

23
00:01:43,665 --> 00:01:46,779
while its height must be adjustable.

24
00:01:48,299 --> 00:01:54,179
This is the head of the virtual prototype with a window cut in it and the

25
00:01:54,199 --> 00:01:56,199
needle train highlighted.

26
00:01:56,599 --> 00:02:00,621
On the front of the main shaft is the needle-bar crank,

27
00:02:00,641 --> 00:02:04,221
with a counterweight cast opposite the crank pin.

28
00:02:04,621 --> 00:02:11,561
The link runs from the crank pin to a connector clamped on the needle bar.

29
00:02:11,961 --> 00:02:16,147
The bar slides in an upper and a lower bushing,

30
00:02:16,167 --> 00:02:21,784
and a guide block on the connector rides in a slot in the head,

31
00:02:21,804 --> 00:02:26,169
so the bar can only move up and down, never turn.

32
00:02:27,689 --> 00:02:31,589
Kinematics: an upside-down slider-crank.

33
00:02:33,109 --> 00:02:42,165
Strip away the shapes and it's an in-line slider-crank: the shaft is crank OA,

34
00:02:42,185 --> 00:02:47,169
the link is AB, the needle bar is slider B.

35
00:02:47,569 --> 00:02:54,509
In mechanics classes the slider-crank is usually an engine.

36
00:02:54,909 --> 00:02:58,978
Careful: the piston is above the crank,

37
00:02:58,998 --> 00:03:05,373
and at the top the crank pin points at it, so OB is r plus l.

38
00:03:05,773 --> 00:03:09,467
The needle bar hangs below the shaft,

39
00:03:09,487 --> 00:03:16,393
and at the top the crank pin points away from it, so OB is l minus r.

40
00:03:16,793 --> 00:03:18,431
The dead centres are swapped.

41
00:03:18,831 --> 00:03:24,567
Copy the engine formula and you get the top and bottom accelerations the

42
00:03:24,587 --> 00:03:25,847
wrong way round.

43
00:03:27,367 --> 00:03:30,427
Now the displacement.

44
00:03:30,827 --> 00:03:36,042
Take top dead centre as zero, downward positive.

45
00:03:36,442 --> 00:03:43,982
From the geometry, OB equals l cos beta minus r cos phi,

46
00:03:44,002 --> 00:03:48,302
and r sin phi equals l sin beta.

47
00:03:48,702 --> 00:03:56,258
Eliminate beta and you get this exact formula, with lambda, r over l,

48
00:03:56,278 --> 00:03:57,905
the link ratio.

49
00:03:58,305 --> 00:04:04,451
Compare it with the piston formula: the second term has the opposite sign,

50
00:04:04,471 --> 00:04:06,285
because the bar hangs.

51
00:04:06,685 --> 00:04:07,933
Try a number:

52
00:04:07,953 --> 00:04:14,667
at 90 degrees of shaft angle the bar has moved only 13.3 millimetres,

53
00:04:14,687 --> 00:04:17,205
less than half the stroke.

54
00:04:18,725 --> 00:04:27,697
This is the mechanism view of Lab 3-1, with the shaft turning steadily.

55
00:04:28,097 --> 00:04:29,445
Watch the needle bar.

56
00:04:29,845 --> 00:04:33,492
At the top it seems to pause for a moment;

57
00:04:33,512 --> 00:04:36,985
at the bottom it turns round in a flash.

58
00:04:37,385 --> 00:04:45,100
At 90 degrees it has moved only 13.3 millimetres, under half the stroke,

59
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:50,365
and its top speed comes at 104.7 degrees, not 90.

60
00:04:50,765 --> 00:04:56,239
The reason is the tilted link: the bigger lambda, the bigger the shift.

61
00:04:57,759 --> 00:05:01,213
Differentiate twice for the acceleration.

62
00:05:01,613 --> 00:05:04,513
Two values are worth remembering.

63
00:05:04,913 --> 00:05:10,413
At the top it's r omega squared times one minus lambda;

64
00:05:10,433 --> 00:05:16,736
at the bottom it's minus r omega squared times one plus lambda,

65
00:05:16,756 --> 00:05:21,653
the larger of the two; their ratio is about 0.56.

66
00:05:22,053 --> 00:05:26,764
Expand the root and the acceleration is the sum of two terms:

67
00:05:26,784 --> 00:05:32,890
a first-order term varying with omega and a second-order term with twice omega,

68
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:34,673
proportional to lambda.

69
00:05:35,073 --> 00:05:41,591
Blue is first order, orange second, yellow the sum, sharpest at the bottom.

70
00:05:43,111 --> 00:05:52,663
The worked example. r is 15.5, l is 55, so lambda is 0.282.

71
00:05:53,063 --> 00:06:04,683
At 5000 r/min omega is about 523.6 radians per second.

72
00:06:05,083 --> 00:06:08,721
The stroke is 31 millimetres,

73
00:06:08,741 --> 00:06:15,658
the peak speed 8.43 metres per second at 104.7 degrees,

74
00:06:15,678 --> 00:06:25,623
and the acceleration at the bottom 5447 metres per second squared, about 555 g.

75
00:06:26,023 --> 00:06:33,240
Check it with the simple dead-centre formula:

76
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:41,763
0.0155 times 523.6 squared times 1.282 is about 5447.

77
00:06:43,283 --> 00:06:48,681
These are the three curves from Lab 3-1: displacement,

78
00:06:48,701 --> 00:06:55,303
velocity and acceleration, with the red line at the current angle.

79
00:06:55,703 --> 00:06:59,534
Near the top the acceleration is flat,

80
00:06:59,554 --> 00:07:07,235
almost unchanged for thirty degrees either side; near the bottom it's sharp.

81
00:07:07,635 --> 00:07:11,792
So the biggest inertia force comes at the bottom,

82
00:07:11,812 --> 00:07:15,202
exactly when the needle is in the cloth,

83
00:07:15,222 --> 00:07:19,550
the loop is forming and the hook is about to catch.

84
00:07:19,950 --> 00:07:25,290
Let the bushings wear and the play grow, and the catch becomes unreliable.

85
00:07:26,810 --> 00:07:29,790
Link ratio and the moment of catching.

86
00:07:31,310 --> 00:07:40,050
In Lecture 2 we said the hook catches when the needle has risen about 2 millimetres.

87
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:48,138
Expand the displacement about bottom dead centre and the rise is roughly r over two,

88
00:07:48,158 --> 00:07:52,084
times one plus lambda, times delta squared.

89
00:07:52,484 --> 00:08:00,002
For our example, 2 millimetres of rise comes at delta of about 26 degrees,

90
00:08:00,022 --> 00:08:03,567
so the catch is near 206.1 degrees.

91
00:08:03,967 --> 00:08:08,421
The formula also says that for the same stroke,

92
00:08:08,441 --> 00:08:13,847
a bigger lambda means a faster rise and an earlier catch.

93
00:08:15,367 --> 00:08:23,227
Now exactly, with r of 15.5 at 5000 r/min.

94
00:08:23,627 --> 00:08:33,677
For lambda of 0.20, 0.282 and 0.40 the 2-millimetre rise comes at 206.9,

95
00:08:33,697 --> 00:08:37,034
206.1 and 204.9 degrees,

96
00:08:37,054 --> 00:08:44,727
while the bottom acceleration climbs from 5099 to 5949.

97
00:08:45,127 --> 00:08:48,225
The catch moves only a degree or two,

98
00:08:48,245 --> 00:08:52,776
but Lecture 2's window is only about ten degrees wide,

99
00:08:52,796 --> 00:08:56,062
so if you change the link or the crank,

100
00:08:56,082 --> 00:09:00,107
re-set the hook angle and the needle-bar height.

101
00:09:01,627 --> 00:09:07,309
On screen the link length sweeps between 35 and 90 millimetres;

102
00:09:07,329 --> 00:09:09,481
watch the curves follow.

103
00:09:09,881 --> 00:09:12,446
A small lambda, a long link,

104
00:09:12,466 --> 00:09:18,726
means less acceleration at the bottom and motion closer to harmonic.

105
00:09:19,126 --> 00:09:24,255
The price is a taller head and longer, heavier parts,

106
00:09:24,275 --> 00:09:29,306
and more reciprocating mass pulls the force back up.

107
00:09:29,706 --> 00:09:37,652
So industrial lockstitch machines mostly sit between 0.25 and 0.32,

108
00:09:37,672 --> 00:09:39,198
a compromise.

109
00:09:39,598 --> 00:09:41,965
In design nothing comes free.

110
00:09:43,485 --> 00:09:46,495
Inertia force and balancing.

111
00:09:48,015 --> 00:09:54,942
The needle bar, clamp and needle, plus the share of the link at the bar end,

112
00:09:54,962 --> 00:09:59,420
make up the reciprocating mass m, here 120 grams.

113
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:03,360
The inertia force is m times a.

114
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:11,685
At 5000 r/min that's about 654 newtons at the bottom.

115
00:10:12,085 --> 00:10:22,385
It goes with speed squared: only 235 at 3000, but 941 at 6000.

116
00:10:22,785 --> 00:10:28,279
A part of a hundred-odd grams carrying hundreds of times its own weight,

117
00:10:28,299 --> 00:10:31,878
back and forth more than eighty times a second.

118
00:10:33,398 --> 00:10:35,848
The inertia force has two parts.

119
00:10:36,248 --> 00:10:39,148
The first-order part has the shaft frequency.

120
00:10:39,548 --> 00:10:45,896
A counterweight on the crank makes a centrifugal force at the same frequency;

121
00:10:45,916 --> 00:10:50,197
it fully balances the rotating parts, crank and pin,

122
00:10:50,217 --> 00:10:55,076
and can cancel part of the first-order reciprocating force.

123
00:10:55,476 --> 00:10:56,496
Why only part?

124
00:10:56,896 --> 00:10:59,235
Its force turns with the crank,

125
00:10:59,255 --> 00:11:04,636
so while it cancels the vertical force it creates a new horizontal one.

126
00:11:05,036 --> 00:11:08,667
The second-order force runs at twice the frequency,

127
00:11:08,687 --> 00:11:11,816
and a counterweight can do nothing about it.

128
00:11:12,216 --> 00:11:17,116
So the best cure for vibration is still a smaller m.

129
00:11:18,636 --> 00:11:22,856
The needle-bar position is a function of shaft angle;

130
00:11:22,876 --> 00:11:26,856
change the speed and the same curve is run faster,

131
00:11:26,876 --> 00:11:30,136
with acceleration going as omega squared.

132
00:11:30,536 --> 00:11:34,679
Robot trajectory planning is exactly the same.

133
00:11:35,079 --> 00:11:40,512
Run the same path in half the time and each joint's speed doubles,

134
00:11:40,532 --> 00:11:46,625
while its acceleration and the inertia torque the motor must supply become

135
00:11:46,645 --> 00:11:48,277
four times as large.

136
00:11:48,677 --> 00:11:54,579
The needle bar's sharp turn at the bottom is like the robot's sharpest corner,

137
00:11:54,599 --> 00:11:57,995
and that's where planners limit acceleration.

138
00:11:59,515 --> 00:12:03,295
An everyday example: a car engine.

139
00:12:03,695 --> 00:12:07,569
Piston, rod and crankshaft are a slider-crank too,

140
00:12:07,589 --> 00:12:10,451
only the piston sits above the crank,

141
00:12:10,471 --> 00:12:14,813
so its dead centres are the reverse of the needle bar's.

142
00:12:15,213 --> 00:12:20,162
In a four-cylinder engine at high revs the second-order force,

143
00:12:20,182 --> 00:12:23,447
at twice crank speed, makes the body hum,

144
00:12:23,467 --> 00:12:27,535
and the counterweights on the crank can't touch it.

145
00:12:27,935 --> 00:12:33,035
So some engines add a pair of balance shafts turning at twice crank speed.

146
00:12:34,555 --> 00:12:35,855
Back to the start.

147
00:12:36,255 --> 00:12:44,615
The bottom acceleration is r omega squared times one plus lambda,

148
00:12:44,635 --> 00:12:49,644
giving about 654 newtons at 5000 r/min.

149
00:12:50,044 --> 00:13:02,097
At 6000 it's 1.44 times as much, about 941, over the 800-newton limit.

150
00:13:02,497 --> 00:13:10,940
Two ways out: keep 6000 and bring the mass under 102 grams,

151
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:19,260
or keep the parts and run no faster than about 5532 r/min.

152
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:26,713
In practice you lighten: a hollow bar, a small clamp, a lightened link,

153
00:13:26,733 --> 00:13:30,000
then re-check balance and oiling.

154
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:37,299
Now Virtual lab 3-1. On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

155
00:13:37,319 --> 00:13:43,687
The numbers in the results panel are blurred here. Reading them is your job.

156
00:13:44,087 --> 00:13:48,285
Task one, change only the link length.

157
00:13:48,305 --> 00:13:57,387
Drag it to 90 millimetres and record the stroke and the dead-centre accelerations.

158
00:13:57,787 --> 00:14:03,662
Then try 35. The curves change shape, and the catching moment moves too.

159
00:14:03,682 --> 00:14:10,048
Task two is to set the link to 40 millimetres and read the new catching angle.

160
00:14:10,448 --> 00:14:15,604
Task three, take the speed from three thousand to six thousand,

161
00:14:15,624 --> 00:14:20,369
and see how many times bigger the peak inertia force gets.

162
00:14:20,389 --> 00:14:22,588
That checks the square law.

163
00:14:22,988 --> 00:14:27,047
Task four, at five thousand revolutions a minute,

164
00:14:27,067 --> 00:14:33,624
adjust the reciprocating mass until the peak force is no more than 400 newtons.

165
00:14:34,024 --> 00:14:38,109
Check every number against your own hand calculation.

166
00:14:38,129 --> 00:14:43,531
The grey dashed curves are the textbook example, there for comparison.

167
00:14:43,551 --> 00:14:47,636
I won't give the answers here. They're yours to find.

168
00:14:48,036 --> 00:14:52,238
The lab comes with a guide and a report template.

169
00:14:52,258 --> 00:14:58,356
Download them on the platform and hand in your report when you're done.

170
00:14:59,876 --> 00:15:06,296
Numbers worked out in design have to be watched with data in the factory.

171
00:15:06,696 --> 00:15:10,940
This is the quality loop in the WenQuest digital factory.

172
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:16,027
The adjustment station measures the hook clearance on every machine,

173
00:15:16,047 --> 00:15:19,768
the readings go over the bus into a control chart,

174
00:15:19,788 --> 00:15:24,555
and when the data drifts the system raises an alarm and drafts a

175
00:15:24,575 --> 00:15:28,820
nonconformance report for a quality inspector to confirm.

176
00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:34,884
You can pick the detection rules yourself, change the drift rate,

177
00:15:34,904 --> 00:15:38,994
and see how many hours earlier the alarm comes.

178
00:15:39,394 --> 00:15:42,727
The second page is the serial-number and batch trace.

179
00:15:42,747 --> 00:15:45,194
From the machine that raised the alarm,

180
00:15:45,214 --> 00:15:50,128
back to the hook batch it was built with and the grinder that made that batch.

181
00:15:50,148 --> 00:15:54,114
Then the other way, to every machine built with the same batch,

182
00:15:54,134 --> 00:15:56,454
and whether any have already shipped.

183
00:15:56,854 --> 00:15:57,832
Today's rule,

184
00:15:57,852 --> 00:16:02,899
re-set the hook and the needle-bar height whenever r or l changes,

185
00:16:02,919 --> 00:16:06,890
is checked and traced like this, machine by machine.

186
00:16:06,910 --> 00:16:10,114
We'll walk through it fully in Lecture 27.

187
00:16:11,634 --> 00:16:17,519
Your assignment has four questions. One, calculation. For a new r, l and speed,

188
00:16:17,539 --> 00:16:22,751
find the stroke, the dead-centre accelerations and the catching angle,

189
00:16:22,771 --> 00:16:24,694
and check them in the lab.

190
00:16:25,094 --> 00:16:28,310
Two, design. Today's shop-floor problem.

191
00:16:28,330 --> 00:16:32,760
Give the numbers for lightening and for limiting speed,

192
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:35,187
and say which you'd recommend.

193
00:16:35,587 --> 00:16:41,442
Three, shorten the link: how do the catch and the bottom acceleration change,

194
00:16:41,462 --> 00:16:46,173
and how should the hook be adjusted? And four, a short answer:

195
00:16:46,193 --> 00:16:51,057
why can't a counterweight fully balance the reciprocating force?

196
00:16:51,457 --> 00:16:57,077
Submit the assignment and the lab report on the WenQuest platform.

197
00:16:58,597 --> 00:17:04,933
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example, at its highest point,

198
00:17:04,953 --> 00:17:08,017
what is the distance from O to B?

199
00:17:08,417 --> 00:17:12,250
Shorten the link, and does the catch come earlier or later?

200
00:17:12,650 --> 00:17:18,816
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

201
00:17:20,336 --> 00:17:22,076
Three things to remember.

202
00:17:22,476 --> 00:17:28,199
One: the needle-bar mechanism is an upside-down slider-crank,

203
00:17:28,219 --> 00:17:35,354
with dead centres swapped relative to an engine, so don't copy the formulas.

204
00:17:35,754 --> 00:17:39,978
Two: the largest acceleration is at the bottom,

205
00:17:39,998 --> 00:17:43,410
r omega squared times one plus lambda;

206
00:17:43,430 --> 00:17:48,016
for a given stroke a bigger lambda catches earlier,

207
00:17:48,036 --> 00:17:52,080
so changing r or l means re-setting the hook.

208
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:55,988
Three: inertia force goes with speed squared,

209
00:17:56,008 --> 00:17:59,673
the counterweight can't touch the second order,

210
00:17:59,693 --> 00:18:02,260
and the surest cure is less mass.

211
